[node name="Text" type="RichTextLabel" parent="UI/DossierModal/Center/Panel/VBox"]
custom_minimum_size = Vector2(0, 380)
layout_mode = 2
theme_override_font_sizes/normal_font_size = 14
bbcode_enabled = true
text = "FACILITY RECORD // PROJECT L.U.R.E. (1974)
FACILITY: Mount Hale Lunar Observatory, Mare Tranquillitatis
OPERATIONS: Lunar Laser Ranging Experiment (LURE)

BACKGROUND:
Following the Apollo 11 deployment of quartz retroreflectors on the lunar surface, Mount Hale was established to conduct high-precision lunar distance ranging using ruby and argon-ion lasers.

INCIDENT SUMMARY:
During an off-axis calibration run during the solar eclipse, an anomalous optical feedback loop locked onto the Coude telescope array. A persistent backscatter return established an optical conduit through the roof shutter, accompanied by localized shadow silhouettes moving against the ambient light.

Standard power shutdown will collapse line voltage while the return conduit is energized, causing an uncontrolled rupture of the pressurized dome envelope. A controlled phase inversion through the primary capacitor banks is required to discharge the loop and seal the dome shutter.

OPTICAL MECHANICS:
• DIRECT ILLUMINATION: The handheld laser beam slows and damages anomalous silhouettes, but sustained direct exposure risks emitter overheat.
• REFLECTED BEAM DISCHARGE: Bouncing the laser beam off optical mirrors or deployable reflectors produces phase-inverted reflected light, rapidly breaking silhouette cohesion.
• EMITTER COOLDOWN: Overheating forces a 3-second thermal reset.
• FLASHLIGHT (F): Toggle suit headlamp to illuminate dark corridors.

PROCEDURE:
1. Recover the alignment laser from the control console.
2. Cycle through Airlock Alpha (Code 1974) and benchmark the Apollo 11 LRRR array at Azimuth 214°.
3. Clear the Cleanroom laser barrier using the Argon-ion wavelength code (488).
4. Energize Capacitor Bays in sequence: Bay A -> Bay C -> Bay B (1 -> 3 -> 2) to prepare the discharge bank.
5. In the Coude Dome, orient the three mirror stations (45°, 135°, 315°) to clear the platform, enter Azimuth Code 8352, and pull the primary discharge lever."

